{
  "dom_id": "step-q159641",
  "name": "Question 3",
  "problem": "It is known that the thickness of ceramic tiles from suppliers X and Y are normally distributed with standard deviations of ${\\sigma }_{x}=1.6\\,\\text{mm}$ and ${\\sigma }_{y}=1.2\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively. A construction contractor wants to determine whether the population mean ${\\mu }_{x}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier X is greater than the population mean ${\\mu }_{y}$ of the thickness of ceramic tiles from supplier Y. He constructs a $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means.\n\nGiven that the contractor sampled $20$ tiles from supplier X and $18$ tiles from supplier Y and found that their mean thicknesses are $18.8\\,\\text{mm}$ and $18.5\\,\\text{mm},$ respectively, which of the following statements are true?\n\nI. The end-points of the $95%$ confidence interval for the difference between the population means are approximately $0.3\\pm 0.894.$\n\nII. All values contained within the $95%$ confidence interval are positive.\n\nIII. The confidence interval data suggests that ${\\mu }_{x}>{\\mu }_{y}.$\n\n*Hint: Given a random variable $Z∼N(0,1),$ the table below shows the $z$-scores ${z}_{p}$ such that $P(Z>{z}_{p})=p$ for some particular values of $p.$*\n\n| $p$ | $0.100$ | $0.050$ | $0.025$ | $0.010$ | $0.005$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| ${z}_{p}$ | $1.282$ | $1.645$ | $1.960$ | $2.326$ | $2.576$ |",
  "worked_solution": "",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
